WEBVTT

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.470
Welcome to the first lesson in our course.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.480
Today, you're going to make three videos.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.840
You'll make the first one before I explain
anything, just so you can see how

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.240
simple this can be. To take you from a
rough idea to a finished video, we'll

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.230
use three tools, each solving a different
part of the process.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.090
First, Seedance 2.5. It creates some of
the most realistic AI video out

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.080
there, footage that can look almost
indistinguishable from a real shoot.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.750
Top studios use it, and what skilled
creators are making with it is

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.600
genuinely mind-blowing. Second,
Supercomputer.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.660
Think of it as a creative partner you can
talk to in plain language.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.210
When you're not sure how to express an
idea, it helps you develop it, add

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.230
detail, and shape it into something the
model can understand.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.120
It combines technical knowledge with
creative judgment, and helps you push

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.220
an idea much further. And finally, a
ready-made preset, it gives you a

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.860
cinematic video in just a couple of
clicks.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.830
All you have to do is upload a photo of
yourself or your character, and

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.470
click generate.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:15.460
Here's how it'll work. I'll do something,
then I'll say pause.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.230
You'll pause the lesson, open the
platform, and do exactly what I just did.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.520
Then you'll come back, and we'll look at
both results, mine and yours.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.660
This part matters. Don't watch this lesson
like a movie from the

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.990
couch. My result won't teach you much,
yours

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.320
will teach you everything.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.760
And let's deal with two fears right away.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.230
I know you have them, I had them too.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.420
The first, there are so many words I don't
understand.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.400
True. And I'll explain every one of them.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.430
More importantly, I'll show you how to
find the answer yourself when I'm not

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.200
around. The second, there are a hundred
models.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.280
I'm going to get confused.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.230
There really are a lot of models.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.860
But think of them as tools in a workshop.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.420
A hammer doesn't compete with a
screwdriver.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.440
Each tool has its own strengths, and every
time we use one, I'll tell you

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.940
which one we're using and why.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.730
You won't have to guess. Two of the three
videos you make today will

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.320
probably be worse than the one you saw in
the intro.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.340
That's normal. That's part of the plan.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.190
The third one will get you there.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.640
Let's start with the simplest possible
request.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:38.130
What happens if we ask the model to create
a sci-fi movie scene in a single

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.120
sentence? No references, no technical
details.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.840
Let's find out. We're going to
Higgsfield.ai.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.620
When you first open the site, you'll see
lots of tabs and information.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.380
It may seem overwhelming at first, but
don't worry, you'll quickly figure

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:58.310
out what's what. Right now, we're looking
for the Cinema Studio tab.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.180
For the moment, all we need is this
control panel, where we'll enter our

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.490
first prompt. This is where you can
generate both images and videos.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.340
Let's switch to the Video tab, select the
C Dense 2.5 model, and quickly go

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.770
through the settings. We'll choose a 21/9
aspect ratio because it looks more

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.540
cinematic. Next, to avoid spending
unnecessary credits, we'll set the

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.380
quality to 720p. That's enough to get a
good result.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.860
We usually use 1080p only when we're
confident in the prompt and the output

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.000
it will produce. Next, we'll set the
duration to 15 seconds and make sure

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.230
we're generating only one video, not four
at once.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.100
We'll leave the sound on and enter our
prompt in the text box.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.680
Create a 15-second video of an epic sci-fi
scene.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:49.360
Before starting the generation, make sure
that one video costs 98 credits.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.020
It's easy to select the wrong settings by
mistake and accidentally spend

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:57.200
more than you intended. Now hit Generate.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.410
Generation usually takes 5 to 10 minutes
and your video will appear here

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.230
when it's ready. Now it's your turn.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.360
Pause the lesson, follow the same steps,
and either enter the exact same

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.660
prompt or try something similar.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.540
Create a 15-second video of an epic sci-fi
scene.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.470
Keep it that simple. Start the generation,
then come back and we'll discuss

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:24.120
what we just did. Look at how simple that
prompt is.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.480
I didn't specify the main character, the
world, the location, or the camera

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.230
movement. I only gave it a format and a
vibe.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.460
This is what I call a lazy prompt.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.940
It gets you moving without forcing you to
write the perfect creative brief

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.760
on your first try. But there's a catch.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:44.410
Anything you leave out, the model decides
for you.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.010
Sometimes it'll surprise you, other times
it'll create something beautiful

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.230
that has nothing to do with what you
imagined.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.440
That's exactly why we're starting with
this one short sentence.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.140
First, we'll see what the model does on
its own, then we'll take the same

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.240
idea and learn how to direct the result.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.200
You're waiting for your generation, and so
am I.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.200
We never just wait, so let's talk credits
and how not to waste

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.270
them. Before you generate, you can always
see exactly

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.600
how many credits it will cost.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.890
Here's your remaining balance, and if you
need more, you can get them here.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.960
It's all on one screen. No surprises.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.680
You can check the details on your own.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.230
The link is in the description.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.140
And I want to be honest about one thing.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.940
A bad attempt still uses credits.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:42.180
You're paying for the generation, not for
whether you like the result.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.240
Think of it like a real shoot.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.980
Once you film a take, the time and
resources are spent, even if that take

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.620
never makes the final cut.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.230
My generation is ready. Let's watch it.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.000
That explosion looks pretty good.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.320
And the little ship flying past this
enormous thing gives you a real sense

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.160
of scale. But follow the laser for a
second.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.120
In one shot, it looks like the big ship is
attacking.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.230
In the next, I can't tell where the beam
is coming from.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:29.730
Then everything explodes. I wanted to
watch one ship outsmart the other.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.640
Instead, I'm trying to work out what just
happened.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.100
And don't get me wrong. For one sentence
that took a few seconds to write,

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.100
this is honestly not bad, but it isn't the
scene I wanted.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.800
Remember that feeling? No, that's not it.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.230
In a moment, it's going to become the most
useful tool in this lesson.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.880
Your first video should be ready by now,
and it deserves a full watch.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.980
Go check it out before we move on.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.950
I'll wait.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:07.160
Now, the same idea, except this time,
we're using a supercomputer.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.230
Back on Higgsfield, click supercomputer in
the top menu.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:14.020
This is supercomputer, not a random
chatbot.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:19.200
It knows how Sea Dance works right now,
and its whole job is to turn

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.940
the idea in your head into language the
model understands.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.270
Think of it as someone next to you who has
made 10,000 videos and never gets

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:33.330
tired. Now I type, "Create a 15-second
video of an epic sci-fi scene

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:38.800
and send it." Sometimes, supercomputer
asks a question or

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.500
two first. Answer in plain language.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.800
What kind of battle? What era?

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.240
What mood? I'll skip the wait.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.730
Let's watch.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.180
Okay, we see the pilot, then the small
ship launches a missile, and look at

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.460
where it hits the larger ship.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.020
It's not just spaceships and explosions,
there's a progression.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.880
The threat, the pilot, the counterattack,
then the payoff.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.080
I asked for an epic space battle.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.240
It worked out how to show one.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.180
Now it's your turn. Now, let's compare the

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.220
results. Left, my one-line prompt.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.580
Right, the same idea through
supercomputer.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.360
The left looks cinematic, but the laser's
direction is confusing.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.280
The big ship explodes, and I'm not sure
who fired the shot.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.820
On the right, we see the pilot, a missile
launch, and the hit that leads to

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.420
the explosion. The action is easier to
follow.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:51.230
That's the whole lesson in one frame.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.980
Not talent, not years of experience, a
clearer prompt.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.960
One more important thing. Sometimes, a
generation starts, but doesn't

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:04.680
produce a finished video. You might see
failed or not safe for

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:08.640
work. It could be a technical issue or a
safety filter.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.090
There's also an intellectual property
check, which looks for protected

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.280
characters and franchise.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.400
Your prompt, or the video itself, can
trigger a block, even if you never

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.940
asked for a famous character.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:25.400
Credits are always returned automatically
for failed or blocked attempts.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.040
Same prompt can give you a different
video.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.440
You're not ordering a pizza, you're
rolling a die, except you get to design

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:36.890
the sides. The better the prompt, the more
sides you'll be happy

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:41.840
with. In this course, we'll teach you how
to reduce that randomness and get

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:47.160
more consistent results. Earlier, we ran
our test generation in 720p to

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.680
save credits instead of going straight to
1080p.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.640
But when I'm trying to get a specific
scene right, or want to test an idea

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.600
with a few different versions, there's a
simple trick to save even more

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:02.530
credits. It's called "Cdense 2.5 Draft".

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:06.830
So I select "Cdense 2.5 Draft" and keep it
at

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:12.440
480p. I'm using the same sci-fi prompt,
but this time I'll

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:15.540
generate four versions in one batch.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:20.430
Each 15 second draft costs 45 credits, so
all four cost

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.510
180.

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:28.390
I can compare the results and pick the one
with the action and camera

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:33.630
movement I like best. If none of them
works, I can adjust the prompt

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:38.520
and try another batch. Once I get a take I
want to keep, I click "Generate"

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:43.710
in 1080p. That costs another 180 credits
and gives me an

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.230
identical result at a higher resolution.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.690
Generating all four directly in 1080p
would cost 720

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:56.190
credits. Four drafts, then taking only my
favorite to 1080p,

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:00.620
costs 360. That's half as much.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.880
Test the idea first. Spend on final
quality once you've found the shot you

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.650
want.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.580
Remember when I said you'd make three
videos today?

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.530
You've made the first two?

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.320
Now, let's make the third, the one you saw
at the beginning.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.220
This time, I'm not writing anything.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.320
Not a single word. This is a ready-made
preset.

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.000
Someone else has already done the hard
part, developed the scene, chosen the

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.780
camera movement, lighting, and sound,
tested dozens of versions, and kept

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:37.480
the best one. Then they packaged it, so
you only have to change one thing,

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:41.360
the main character. All you need is one
image.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.780
It can be a photo of you or a friend,
watch the clicks, and follow along.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:51.110
I open the preset, I upload the image
here, I check that the face is clearly

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.830
visible, then I click "Generate".

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.220
Take a second and think about that.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:14.380
Ten minutes ago, you didn't know what a
prompt was.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:19.060
Now you have a cinematic video with you or
your character in the

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.610
lead. Pause here, choose any image, and
use this

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:25.150
preset.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:30.680
Simple. Everything I did today, you do
with your own hands.

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:35.600
Three videos, the lazy prompt, the same
prompt through supercomputer, and

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:38.540
the preset with your own image.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.640
Apply your homework in the window below,
and that's it.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.380
One more thing before you go.

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:47.840
What you just made in ten minutes used to
take a crew and a budget.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:51.230
Not because you're a genius, the tool
changed.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:54.900
When a tool changes this much, a short
window opens.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.480
Far fewer people can use it than need it.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.980
We're inside that window. Want to go
deeper?

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:05.540
You'll find a PDF below with more on
sedents and supercomputer.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:09.080
In the next lesson, you're going to get a
second brain.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:13.840
That's not a metaphor. It's a real tool
that will completely change how you

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.230
work with the platform. You'll see.
